  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.6 z^2) / 2, drawn as a surface at half the loss. It is steep along x (left to right), so overshoot starts there: plain steps swing back and forth once the rate passes 1/2.4 ≈ 0.42 and grow when it passes 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83.",
    "Step = -rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with mean zero and size 1.2 × sqrt((64 − b) / (63 b)), like sampling b of 64 points without putting them back. It is exactly zero at b = 64.",
    "Both arrows are steps (rate × slope), not the slope itself. They are drawn 4 times their real horizontal length on the same scale, along the surface. The purple one is the step the ball takes next; the dark one is the step all 64 points would give.",
    "Diverged: the loss goes over 5 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl, and the run stops. The ball is then drawn at the rim, and the loss readout keeps its true value. Otherwise a run finishes after 40 steps.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus one point per step (up to 40 steps). Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the first run's noise. Each new run uses the next part of the sequence.",
    "With noise, a single step lowers the loss only on average. With small batches, it keeps wandering near the lowest point instead of settling on it."
  ]
}